1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
 In this lesson, I will talk about prompt files in GitHub Copilot.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,360
 Let's get started.

3
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:10,200
 Prompt files are like saved conversations with Copilot.

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:14,000
 Instead of typing the same instructions over and over again,

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,000
 for example, generate the React form,

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,400
 or review this API for security issues.

7
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:25,000
 You can save that prompt into a file and reuse it anytime.

8
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:31,000
 Each prompt file is written in markdown and has the extension.prom.md.

9
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:34,000
 You can save it as your personal library of smart tasks,

10
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,000
 always ready to run whenever you need them.

11
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,000
 Unlike custom instructions which apply everywhere, prompt files are on-demand.

12
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,000
 You use them only when you trigger them,

13
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,000
 which makes them perfect for repeatable workflows.

14
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,000
 Every prompt file has two main parts.

15
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:57,000
 At the top, there is an optional YML header where you can describe what the prompt does.

16
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:03,000
 For example, generate the React component or perform code review.

17
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:09,000
 Here you can also set which model Copilot should use and which tools are available for that prompt.

18
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:13,000
 You can even pick the chat mode like ask, edit or agent.

19
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:18,000
 Then comes the main part, the prompt body written in markdown.

20
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:27,000
 This is where you tell Copilot what to do, add instructions, or link to other files in your workspace.

21
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:33,000
 You can also insert dynamic values using variable such as selection for selected text,

22
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:39,000
 file base name for the current file, or input component name for user input.

23
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:44,000
 This lets you prompt automatically adapt to whatever you're working on.

24
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,000
 You can save prompt files in two different places.

25
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:54,000
 If you want them to work on the in your current project, place them inside the .gethub/promp folder.

26
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,000
 These are called workspace prompt files.

27
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,000
 If you want them to be available across all projects, create them in your user profile.

28
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:05,000
 Those are called user prompt files.

29
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:12,000
 You can even sync them between devices using set in sync, so your personal prompt library always stays up to date.

30
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,000
 When writing your own prompts, always explain clearly what you expect from Copilot.

31
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:26,000
 Describe the goal, show example input, and output, and a link to any instruction files instead of repeating the same text.

32
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:37,000
 If you often build similar features or perform code reviews, prompt files can save you a lot of time and help keep your workflow consistent across all projects.

33
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:44,000
 Now you know how to use prompt files in GitHub Copilots to create repeatable and reusable tasks.

34
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,000
 You can build your own library of coding prompts,

35
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:56,000
 regenerate in components, writing tests, or reviewing code, and run them anytime with just one command.

36
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:01,000
 I hope this lesson was useful for you and see you in the next lesson.

